==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|constituten}}, from {{der|en|la|cōnstitūtum}}, neuter of {{m|la|cōnstitūtus}}, past participle of {{der|en|la|cōnstituō|t=to put in place, set up, establish}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkɒnstɪtjuːt/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkɑnstɪt(j)uːt/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-uk-constitute.ogg|a=UK}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[set up]]; to [[establish]]; to [[enact]].
#* {{RQ:Taylor Holy Living|passage=Laws appointed and '''constituted''' by lawful authority.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[make up]]; to [[compose]]; to [[form]].
#* '''1779–81''', {{w|Samuel Johnson}}, "Abraham Cowley" in ''Lives of the Most Eminent English Poet''
#*: Truth and reason '''constitute''' that intellectual gold that defies destruction.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[appoint]], depute, or elect to an office; to make and empower.
#* {{RQ:Wordsworth The Excursion|passage=Me didst Thou '''constitute''' a priest of thine.}}

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|set up}} {{l|en|establish}}, {{l|en|enact}}
* {{sense|make up}} {{l|en|make up}}, {{l|en|compose}}, {{l|en|form}}; see also [[Thesaurus:compose]]
* {{sense|appoint}}

====Related terms====
